.Dd May 31, 2026
.Dt UMERGE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm umerge
.Nd interactive two-way and three-way directory comparison
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl m Ar tool
.Op Fl A | Fl U
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl I
.Ar left right
.Nm
.Op Fl m Ar tool
.Op Fl A | Fl U
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl I
.Ar left parent right
.Nm
.Fl h
.Nm
.Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a terminal user interface for comparing two or three directory trees
side by side.
It recursively enumerates the given directories,
matches entries by name in a case-sensitive sorted merge,
and runs
.Xr diff 1
or
.Xr diff3 1
on each matched file pair in the background.
Comparison results are displayed as they arrive;
the interface remains fully navigable while comparison is in progress.
.Pp
In two-way mode
.Nm
accepts two directories,
.Ar left
and
.Ar right ,
and displays them in side-by-side columns separated by a vertical bar.
.Pp
In three-way mode
.Nm
accepts three directories:
.Ar left ,
the first child tree;
.Ar parent ,
the common ancestor displayed in the center column;
and
.Ar right ,
the second child tree.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl m Ar tool , Fl -merge Ns = Ns Ar tool
Select the external diff and merge tool.
.Ar tool
must be one of:
.Bl -tag -width "emacs  " -compact
.It vim
Use
.Xr vim 1
to view single files and
.Xr vimdiff 1
to diff two or three files.
This is the default.
.It emacs
Use
.Xr emacs 1
to view single files,
.Sy ediff-files
to diff two files,
and
.Sy ediff3
to diff three files.
.El
.It Fl A , Fl -ascii
Use ASCII tree symbols
.Pq Sy > No / Sy v
instead of the Unicode default
.Pq Sy ▶ No / Sy ▼ .
Useful on terminals that render the Unicode symbols at the wrong column
width, shifting the display (their East Asian Width is "Ambiguous," so
this varies by terminal).
.It Fl U , Fl -unicode
Use Unicode tree symbols
.Pq Sy ▶ No / Sy ▼ .
This is the default;
.Fl -unicode
is provided for explicitness or to override a future config-file setting.
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl -ascii .
.It Fl r , Fl -read-only
Disable the copy
.Pq Ic a , Ic b , Ic c
and delete
.Pq Ic d
key bindings, plus the three-way merge
.Pq Ic m , Ic M , Ic n
and mark-resolved
.Pq Ic R
key bindings; only navigation, collapsing/expanding, and viewing in the
configured diff/merge tool remain active.
Intended for use as a
.Xr git 1
.Cm difftool
backend
.Pq see Sx GIT INTEGRATION below :
that mode presents whichever side matches your actual working tree as
symbolic links back into it rather than copies, so without
.Fl -read-only ,
.Ic d
would silently unlink the symlink without touching the real file, and
.Ic a Ns / Ns Ic b Ns / Ns Ic c
would follow it and genuinely overwrite the real file.
.It Fl I , Fl -no-gitignore
Don't skip entries matched by a top-level
.Pa .gitignore
in any compared root
.Pq see Sx GITIGNORE FILTERING below .
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print version and copyright information to standard output and exit 0.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit 0.
.El
.Sh DISPLAY
The terminal is divided into two or three equal-width columns separated
by vertical bars.
The header row shows the absolute path of each root directory.
A status bar at the bottom of the screen shows the current cursor
position, whether background comparison is still running, and a summary
of available key bindings.
.Ss Tree symbols
Each directory entry is preceded by a two-character symbol, by default the
Unicode
.Sy ▶
/
.Sy ▼
triangles
.Pq Fl -ascii No falls back to Sy > No / Sy v :
.Bl -tag -width "▶ (unicode) / > (ascii)  "
.It ▶ No (unicode) / Sy > No (ascii)
The directory is collapsed and its children are not shown.
.It ▼ No (unicode) / Sy v No (ascii)
The directory is expanded and its children are visible below it.
.It (two spaces)
The entry is a regular file.
.El
Entries are indented two spaces per level of depth.
.Ss Colors
.Bl -tag -width "Blue  "
.It White
The entry is present on all sides.
File contents are identical, or comparison has not yet completed.
.It Blue
The entry is present on all sides and file contents differ.
In three-way mode only the columns adjacent to a differing pair are
highlighted: if
.Ar left
differs from
.Ar parent ,
the left and center columns are blue; if
.Ar right
differs from
.Ar parent ,
the center and right columns are blue.
Both pairs may be blue simultaneously.
.It Green
The entry is absent from at least one side.
Columns where it is present are highlighted green; absent columns are
blank.
.El
.Ss Selection
Pressing
.Ic s
.Pq see Sx KEY BINDINGS
toggles selection on the entry at the cursor, propagating to its whole
subtree.
A selected entry is marked with a one-character glyph, black text on a
yellow background, in a one-character gutter to the left of the tree
symbols: by default the Unicode
.Sy ●
.Pq Fl -ascii No falls back to Sy * .
The marker reflects selection only; the entry's own color
.Pq white/blue/green/red
is left unchanged, so a selected entry's diff status is still visible.
The gutter shows the marker once per row, not once per column, since
selection applies to the whole entry regardless of how many sides it
exists on.
.Pp
Selection is tree-wide: when one or more entries are selected,
.Ic a , b , c ,
and
.Ic d
act on every selected entry instead of just the one at the cursor
.Pq see Sx KEY BINDINGS .
The status bar shows a
.Dq Ar N No selected
count whenever the selection is non-empty, since a selected entry can be
scrolled out of view.
The selection is not cleared by a bulk copy — this lets the same
selection be copied to a second destination in three-way mode without
reselecting; press
.Ic Esc
to clear it explicitly when you're done with it.
.Pp
Selecting or deselecting a directory always applies to
.Em all
of its current descendants, with no exceptions: a directory's selected
state can never lie about what is underneath it.
Consequently, toggling
.Ic s
on an entry whose ancestor is already selected is a no-op — a status-bar
message explains that the containing directory must be deselected first.
To select all but one entry in a directory, deselect the directory and
select the other entries individually.
.Pp
A hidden entry
.Po
see
.Ic h Ns / Ns Ic H
in
.Sx KEY BINDINGS
.Pc
rendered while
.Ic H
is on keeps whichever of the colors above it would otherwise have, but
much darker, with medium-gray text
.Pq legible against the dark background regardless of category .
The cursor row always looks the same regardless of whether the entry
under it is hidden; move the cursor off an entry to see whether it is
hidden.
.Ss Resolution status
In three-way mode, each entry carries a one-character resolution-status
marker next to its name, tracking where it stands in the merge workflow
.Pq see Sx KEY BINDINGS Ic m , Ic M , Ic n , Ic R :
.Bl -tag -width "(blank)  "
.It (blank)
Unresolved: no merge action has been taken yet.
Colored green.
.It Ic a No / Sy b
Resolved by copying
.Ar left
/
.Ar right
into
.Ar parent .
Colored green.
.It Ic m
Auto-merged cleanly with
.Xr diff3 1 Ns 's
.Fl m
mode: the changes on both sides did not overlap.
Colored yellow.
.It Ic r
Manually resolved: the user pressed
.Ic R
after fixing a conflict by hand.
Colored yellow.
.It Ic c
Conflict: needs manual resolution.
Colored red.
.El
A directory's own marker is left blank when its children have a mix of
statuses, since no single character can honestly summarize that; expand
it to see each child's own status.
.Ss Diff counts
Once a file comparison completes, a count is appended after the filename:
.Bl -tag -width "= "
.It =
Zero differences; files are identical.
.It Ar n
Files differ in
.Ar n
hunks.
.It bin
The files differ and at least one side is binary content; a hunk count
does not apply, since
.Nm
never runs
.Xr diff 1 Ns / Ns Xr diff3 1
on binary files
.Po
it detects this itself first, the same way
.Xr git 1
does: checking whether the leading bytes contain a NUL byte
.Pc .
This entry is still highlighted the same as any other difference.
.El
In two-way mode the count appears in the left column.
In three-way mode the left column shows the count of hunks between
.Ar left
and
.Ar parent ,
and the right column shows the count between
.Ar right
and
.Ar parent .
.Sh KEY BINDINGS
.Bl -tag -width "Page Down  "
.It Ic Up , k
Move the cursor up one row.
Scrolls the view if the cursor is at the top of the viewport.
.It Ic Down , j
Move the cursor down one row.
Scrolls the view if the cursor is at the bottom of the viewport.
.It Ic Page Up
Scroll the view up one page.
The cursor is clamped to remain within the new viewport.
.It Ic Page Down
Scroll the view down one page.
The cursor is clamped to remain within the new viewport.
.It Ic Left , Right
Collapse or expand the directory at the cursor.
Has no effect on file entries.
.It Ic Enter
If the cursor is on a directory, collapse or expand it.
.Pp
If the cursor is on a file,
.Nm
suspends its display, opens the matched file or files in the configured
diff/merge tool (see
.Fl m ) ,
and resumes when the tool exits.
The command invoked depends on how many sides have the file:
.Bl -tag -width "Three files  " -offset indent -compact
.It One file
.Xr vim 1
.Ar file
(or
.Xr emacs 1
.Ar file )
.It Two files
.Xr vimdiff 1
.Ar left right
(or
.Sy ediff-files )
.It Three files
.Xr vimdiff 1
.Ar left parent right
(or
.Sy ediff3 )
.El
.Pp
When the tool is
.Xr vimdiff 1 ,
its diff highlight groups
.Pq Ic DiffChange , Ic DiffText , Ic DiffAdd , Ic DiffDelete
are recolored to match
.Nm Ns 's
own tree palette
.Pq changed entries blue, present-on-one-side entries green ,
the same way Araxis Merge uses one consistent color scheme across its
directory and file comparison views, instead of
.Xr vim 1 Ns 's
unrelated defaults.
This only affects the launched session, not the user's
.Pa .vimrc .
.Pp
.Sy ediff
gets the same treatment: its
.Ic ediff-odd-diff- Ns Ar X
and
.Ic ediff-even-diff- Ns Ar X
faces
.Pq Ar X No being A, B, or C
are recolored to the same blue — every difference in the buffer gets
this color, since
.Sy ediff
has no equivalent of
.Ic DiffAdd
(a region present in only one buffer): edit or one-sided insertion
alike, every difference uses the same faces.
.Sy ediff Ns 's
own emphasis for the difference you are currently on
.Pq Ic ediff-current-diff- Ns Ar X
and its word-level highlighting within a hunk
.Pq Ic ediff-fine-diff- Ns Ar X
are deliberately left alone: testing found neither actually renders
distinctly in a terminal session, only in a graphical frame, so
theming them would have no visible effect.
.Nm
always launches
.Sy emacs
with
.Fl nw ,
so it stays in the invoking terminal rather than opening a new window —
otherwise plain
.Xr emacs 1
pops a separate graphical frame whenever a display is available, which
would break the
.Dq suspend the display, run the tool, resume
model this section describes.
This only affects the launched session, not the user's
.Pa .emacs
or
.Pa init.el .
.Pp
The entry is automatically re-compared when the tool exits, in case it
was edited.
.It Ic s
Toggle selection on the entry at the cursor, propagating to its whole
subtree
.Pq see Sx Selection under Sx DISPLAY .
When one or more entries are selected,
.Ic a , b , c ,
and
.Ic d
below act on the whole selection instead of just the entry at the
cursor.
.It Ic Esc
Clear the whole selection, tree-wide, regardless of cursor position.
Selection otherwise persists after a bulk copy, so this is the way to
deliberately start over once you're done with it.
.It Ic a
With no selection, in two-way mode, copy the entry at the cursor from
.Ar left
to
.Ar right .
.Pp
In three-way mode, prompts
.Dq Copy from A (left) to:
press
.Ic b
to copy to
.Ar right
or
.Ic c
to copy to
.Ar parent .
Any other key cancels.
.Pp
With a selection, the same two-way/three-way behavior applies to every
selected entry instead of the cursor entry; an entry in the selection
that is absent on the source side is silently skipped rather than
aborting the whole operation.
.It Ic b
With no selection, in two-way mode, copy the entry at the cursor from
.Ar right
to
.Ar left .
.Pp
In three-way mode, prompts
.Dq Copy from B (right) to:
press
.Ic a
to copy to
.Ar left
or
.Ic c
to copy to
.Ar parent .
Any other key cancels.
.Pp
With a selection, acts on every selected entry instead of the cursor
entry, same as
.Ic a
above.
.It Ic c
Three-way mode only.
Prompts
.Dq Copy from C (parent) to:
press
.Ic a
to copy to
.Ar left
or
.Ic b
to copy to
.Ar right .
Any other key cancels.
With a selection, acts on every selected entry instead of the cursor
entry, same as
.Ic a
above.
.Pp
For
.Ic a , b ,
and
.Ic c :
copying onto a destination that already exists replaces it outright
(an existing destination directory is removed first, rather than merged
into); there is no confirmation prompt.
.It Ic d
With no selection, delete the entry at the cursor from every side on
which it exists.
With a selection, delete every selected entry instead.
There is no confirmation prompt either way.
.It Ic r
Re-enumerate the entry at the cursor from disk
.Pq directories only; a file has nothing to enumerate
and re-compare it, in the background — the same mechanism used for the
initial comparison.
Picks up changes made outside
.Nm
since it started (files added, removed, or edited by another program).
Does nothing but flash a message if another comparison is already running.
.It Ic h
Toggle the hidden flag on the entry at the cursor, propagating to its
whole subtree.
This is a user-managed
.Dq ignore this
flag, unrelated to dot-files or
.Sx GITIGNORE FILTERING
below.
Hidden entries drop out of the tree view immediately unless
.Ic H
is on.
A descendant can later be un-hidden on its own without disturbing an
already-hidden ancestor's flag; reaching it to do so requires
.Ic H
first, since a hidden entry's cursor position no longer exists once it
drops out of view.
.It Ic H
Toggle whether hidden entries are shown at all.
When on, hidden entries are rendered in a much darker shade of their
usual color, with medium-gray text, rather than omitted
.Pq e.g. a hidden-but-changed entry still shows blue, just a dark navy
instead of its usual pale blue .
.It Ic m
Three-way mode only.
Auto-merge the entry at the cursor into
.Ar parent ,
regardless of any active selection
.Po
unlike
.Ic a , b , c , d ,
this is not selection-aware; use
.Ic M
for the selection
.Pc .
See
.Sx Resolution status
under
.Sx DISPLAY
for the classifier this applies.
.It Ic M
Three-way mode only.
Auto-merge every selected entry into
.Ar parent .
Flashes a message if nothing is selected.
.It Ic n
Three-way mode only.
Auto-merge the entire tree into
.Ar parent
in one keystroke, regardless of cursor position or selection.
.It Ic R
Three-way mode only.
Mark the entry at the cursor's whole subtree as resolved
.Pq status Ic r .
Does not itself re-run any comparison or merge; use after resolving a
conflict by hand in the diff/merge tool.
.It Ic q , Ctrl-C
Quit
.Nm .
.El
.Sh GITIGNORE FILTERING
By default,
.Nm
reads a top-level
.Pa .gitignore
from each compared root
.Po
.Ar left ,
.Ar parent ,
and
.Ar right ,
whichever are present
.Pc ,
combines their patterns, and skips any matching entry entirely — it is
never enumerated, compared, or shown.
.Pa .git
itself is always skipped this way too, even with no
.Pa .gitignore
present.
.Pp
Only the top-level
.Pa .gitignore
is read; patterns in
.Pa .gitignore
files nested in subdirectories are not yet honored.
Pass
.Fl I , Fl -no-gitignore
to disable this filtering entirely.
.Sh GIT INTEGRATION
.Xr git 1 Ns 's
own directory-diff mode
.Po
.Cm git difftool -d ,
also
.Cm --dir-diff
.Pc
materializes two temporary directories and invokes one external command
with two paths — the same calling convention
.Nm
already uses, so it works as a
.Cm difftool
backend:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[difftool "umerge"]
    cmd = umerge --read-only "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"
[diff]
    tool = umerge
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Fl -read-only
is deliberate.
In this mode, git presents whichever side matches the actual working tree
as symbolic links back into it, rather than copies, to avoid duplicating
bytes already on disk.
Without
.Fl -read-only ,
.Ic d
would silently unlink that symlink without touching the real file, while
.Ic a Ns / Ns Ic b Ns / Ns Ic c
would follow it and genuinely overwrite the real working-tree file — with
no visual indication that anything outside the temporary diff session was
touched.
.Fl -read-only
disables all of these (plus the three-way merge and mark-resolved key
bindings) so the git integration is a safe viewer by default.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr diff 1 ,
.Xr diff3 1 ,
.Xr emacs 1 ,
.Xr vim 1 ,
.Xr vimdiff 1
